src/compiler.js (1200 lines): same Compiler class, same compile_* method per node type, same buffer/scope-closer bookkeeping, same tmpid scheme — but the emitted source is Perl. Root shape per ADR 0003: sub root ($env, $context, $frame, $runtime, $cb) with lineno/colno tracking, try/catch → handle_error → $cb->($err), blocks as b_<name>, return of { root, b_* }. Emitted code calls Runtime for every operator whose JS semantics differ from Perl's (+, ==, truthiness, comparison, in) — never inline Perl operators for those. String literals emitted safely (no interpolation holes: think '$', '@', '\\'). Keep the emitted source readable; it is what users debug. Port per skill text-nunjucks-core: same functions, same order, snake_case names, upstream control flow kept. Probe surprises with the oracle and commit them as corpus cases. Any deliberate difference needs an ADR in the same change.

FORWARD NOTES from k14 (runtime) audit: (1) regular-for compiler MUST emit a JS-truthiness guard equivalent to upstream if(arr) (compiler.js:664) -- use $runtime->_truthy($arr), NOT 'if defined', else runtime.keys(None) crashes (same root as async-loop bug F2). (2) suppress_value faithfully returns the RAW value when autoescape is off, so the output path must append via $runtime->_js_to_string(...), NOT Perl '.' concat, or numbers stringify with %.15g instead of number_to_string. (3) The runtime operator surface (op_add/sub/mul/div/mod/pow/floordiv/neg/pos/concat, op_eq/ne/seq/sne/lt/gt/le/ge, op_and/or/not, _truthy/_falsy) is complete and matches compiler.js emission -- call these, never native Perl operators.

